<article class="env-example" id="00SR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00SR">Example <span data-tag="00SR">10.136.8</span>.</a> Consider the ring map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \begin{eqnarray*}  R = \mathbf{Z}[a_1, \ldots , a_ n] &  \longrightarrow &  S = \mathbf{Z}[\alpha _1, \ldots , \alpha _ n] \\  a_1 &  \longmapsto &  \alpha _1 + \ldots + \alpha _ n \\  \ldots &  \ldots &  \ldots \\  a_ n &  \longmapsto &  \alpha _1 \ldots \alpha _ n \end{eqnarray*}
</div>
  <p> In other words this is the unique ring map of polynomial rings as indicated such that </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  x^ n + a_1 x^{n - 1} + \ldots + a_ n = \prod \nolimits _{i = 1}^ n (x + \alpha _ i)  \]
</div>
  <p> holds in $\mathbf{Z}[\alpha _ i, x]$. Another way to say this is that $a_ i$ maps to the $i$th elementary symmetric function in $\alpha _1, \ldots , \alpha _ n$. By the usual theory of elementary symmetric polynomials (details omitted) the ring $S$ is finite free over $R$ with basis the elements $\alpha _1^{e_1} \alpha _2^{e_2} \ldots \alpha _ n^{e_ n}$ with $0 \leq e_ i \leq n - i$. A fortiori, the fibre rings of $R \to S$ are finite and hence have dimension $0$. On the other hand, $S$ is generated by $n$ elements over $R$ subject to $n$ equations. Hence $S$ is a relative global complete intersection over $R$. Since the rank of $S$ over $R$ is positive, we also see that $S$ is faithfully flat over $R$. </p>
</article>